     1	"""kpharness: boot a headless game, ask it things, run scenarios, tear down.
     2	
     3	  kpharness boot   --target k1            stage, boot, connect, leave running
     4	  kpharness status [--target k1]          discovery facts, or `status` if booted
     5	  kpharness state  [--target k1]          the engine state snapshot
     6	  kpharness reply  --target k1 INDEX      choose one current dialog reply
     7	  kpharness exec   [--target k1] CODE     run a Lua chunk over the channel
     8	  kpharness events [--target k1]          events since a cursor
     9	  kpharness shots  [--target k1]          take one screenshot now
    10	  kpharness run    SCENARIO [--target k1] boot, execute, tear down, report
    11	  kpharness down   [--target k1]          tear a booted session down
    12	
    13	  kpharness pioneer init   --out R.toml --target k1 --name "..."
    14	  kpharness pioneer step   --out R.toml '{"do": "lua", "code": "..."}'
    15	  kpharness pioneer commit --out R.toml
    16	  kpharness pioneer discard --out R.toml
    17	  kpharness pioneer status --out R.toml
    18	    A CLI TAS: pioneer a route on a booted session one verified step at a
    19	    time. `step` runs the given step body now over the real dispatch path a
    20	    scenario run uses and prints the result; `commit` appends exactly that
    21	    already-inspected step to R.toml. Nothing is written to the route file
    22	    on a whim -- only what was just executed and looked at.
    23	
    24	  kpharness pioneer attach      --out R.toml
    25	  kpharness pioneer play        --out R.toml [--until N] [--verify-current LUA | --restart]
    26	  kpharness pioneer fastforward --out R.toml --speed X [--until N] ...
    27	  kpharness pioneer stop        --out R.toml
    28	  kpharness pioneer reclaim     --out R.toml
    29	    Replay committed steps through one attached owner process, which is
    30	    then the only engine connection. While it is attached, `step`, `commit`
    31	    and `discard` for that route go through it, and `exec`, `reply`,
    32	    `state`, `events` and `shots` are refused. `--speed` is the engine's
    33	    configured multiplier in (0,16], read back before it is reported; the
    34	    prior multiplier is restored at job end unless a route `speed` step ran.
    35	    `stop` (or SIGTERM/SIGHUP to the owner) stops execution. It does not
    36	    pause the game world. `reclaim` recovers after an owner was killed
    37	    without cleanup; it refuses while the owner still holds its locks.
    38	
    39	A booted session leaves tmp/<target>_harness/session.json behind; the
    40	other commands find the port there. `run` does the whole cycle itself.
    41	Exit status: 0 on success, 1 on a scenario or command failure, 2 on a boot
    42	failure, 3 on a usage error.
